    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Celtic settlement of Medhelanon was taken by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> in 222 BCE; as Mediolanum it became an imperial seat under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximian">Maximian</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edict-of-milan">Edict of Milan</a> was issued from it in 313.</li><li>After the destruction of 1162 and a long struggle between the della Torre and the Visconti, the Visconti became dukes in 1395 and the Sforza took the city in 1450, before Spanish, Austrian and French rule.</li><li>The Five Days of 1848 and the defeat of the Austrians in 1859 brought Milan into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy</a>, and in the 19th and 20th centuries it became the country’s industrial and financial centre.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Under the cathedral lie the ruins of the ancient church of Saint Tecla and the baptistery where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustine-of-hippo">Augustine of Hippo</a> was baptised.</p></aside>
    </section>

