    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 590 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">A Celtic tribe of the Insubres settles Medhelanon</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">222 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> under the consul <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gnaeus-cornelius-scipio-calvus">Gnaeus Cornelius Scipio Calvus</a> capture the settlement</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">313</span><span class="rf-text">Constantine issues the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edict-of-milan">Edict of Milan</a> from Mediolanum</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1162</span><span class="rf-text">Frederick I Barbarossa brings the destruction of much of the city</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1450</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francesco-sforza">Francesco Sforza</a> conquers Milan and ends the Ambrosian Republic</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1629</span><span class="rf-text">The Great Plague of Milan begins and kills an estimated 60,000 people by 1631</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1848</span><span class="rf-text">The Five Days rebellion forces Radetzky to withdraw temporarily</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1859</span><span class="rf-text">After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-solferino">Battle of Solferino</a> Milan and the rest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a> are incorporated into Piedmont-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Milan stands in the north-western part of the Po Valley, about halfway between the river Po to the south and the foothills of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> and the great lakes to the north, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ticino">Ticino</a> to the west and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adda">Adda</a> to the east. The land is flat and its highest point is 122 m above sea level. Five watercourses lay near the site: the Seveso and the Lambro to the east, and the Pudiga, the Nirone and the Olona to the west. Three hypotheses are offered for the choice of place: a line of springs on a spit of land that overlooked a swamp, the five watercourses, or an existing Celtic sanctuary near the modern Piazza della Scala.</p><p>Around 590 BCE a Celtic tribe of the Insubres group, belonging to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golasecca-culture">Golasecca culture</a>, settled the place as Medhelanon. In the legend reported by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a>, the Gaulish king Ambicatus sent his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bellovesus">Bellovesus</a> into northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, and Bellovesus allegedly founded the settlement in the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarquinius-priscus">Tarquinius Priscus</a>. The settlement grew around a sanctuary, a wooded ellipse with a central clearing and axes of 443 m and 323 m, aligned on the heliacal risings of Antares and Capella. About two centuries later houses were built around it, a temple of the goddess Belisama and a fortified building with a moat. Excavation under the Roman forum found wooden houses of the 5th century BCE, and near the Royal Palace the remains of houses and a furnace of the 5th and 4th centuries BCE.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/maximian"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maximian.jpg" alt="Maximian" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/maximian">Maximian</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mus%C3%A9e_Saint-Raymond_-_2017-09-02_-_Inv._Ra_34b_-_4654.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Pierre-Selim, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Roman Mediolanum became the most important centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cisalpine-gaul">Cisalpine Gaul</a> and a hub in the road network of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, and in 49 BCE it became a municipium. In the Augustan age it was famous for its schools and had a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> and an amphitheatre of 129.5 by 109.3 m, the third largest in Roman Italy. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximian">Maximian</a>, left at Milan by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a>, enlarged the city to 375 acres inside a new stone wall of about 4.5 km, and built a circus of 470 by 85 m, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bath">Baths</a> of Hercules and a large complex of imperial palaces. In the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambrose">Ambrose</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-i">Theodosius I</a> the city reached the height of its ancient power.</p><p>From the 12th century until the 16th Milan was one of the largest European cities. A period of peace followed the destruction of 1162, and the city prospered as a centre of trade because of its position; its chief industries were armaments and wool. Under the Sforza mulberry growing and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> processing developed, the Sforza Castle and the Ospedale Maggiore were completed, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonardo-da-vinci">Leonardo da Vinci</a> worked in the city from 1482 until 1499.</p><p>After unification a railway network made Milan the rail hub of northern Italy, and with the Gotthard tunnel of 1882 and the Simplon tunnel of 1906 a major rail hub of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. The first power station in continental Europe opened there in 1883. The population rose from 1.3 million in 1951 to 1.7 million in 1967 and peaked at 1,743,427 in 1973.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/diocletian"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/diocletian.jpg" alt="Diocletian" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Istanbul_Archaeology_Museum_Bust_of_Diocletian,_from_Nikomedia,_Izmit,_end_3rd_century_AD_1536.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Dosseman, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> took the settlement in 222 BCE. In 286 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a> moved the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-roman-empire">Western Roman Empire</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> to Mediolanum, leaving his colleague <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximian">Maximian</a> there, and in 402 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honorius">Honorius</a> moved the imperial residence to Ravenna. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostrogoths">Ostrogoths</a> held the city until 539, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a> conquered it in the summer of 569; they made Ticinum their capital in 572 and left Milan to the governance of its archbishops. It surrendered to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> in 774.</p><p>The commune emerged in the 11th century and was confirmed after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-constance">Peace of Constance</a> in 1183. In 1259 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martino-della-torre">Martino della Torre</a> was elected Capitano del Popolo and ruled with autocratic powers. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottone-visconti">Ottone Visconti</a>, made archbishop in 1262, won the city for his family at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-desio">Battle of Desio</a> in 1277. In 1395 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gian-galeazzo-visconti">Gian Galeazzo Visconti</a> became the first Duke of Milan. After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/filippo-maria-visconti">Filippo Maria Visconti</a> in 1447 the Ambrosian Republic followed, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francesco-sforza">Francesco Sforza</a> conquered the city in 1450.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-wars">Italian Wars</a> brought French rule from 1500 until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-pavia">Battle of Pavia</a> in 1525, and the Sforza line ended in 1535. The city then passed to the Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>, and in 1713 and 1714 the Treaties of Utrecht and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rastatt">Rastatt</a> confirmed Austrian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>. Milan was the capital of the Napoleonic Cisalpine Republic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy</a> from 1797, and of Lombardy-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venetia">Venetia</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> from 1815. It joined Piedmont-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> in 1859 and the Kingdom of Italy in 1861.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/la-scala"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/la-scala.jpg" alt="La Scala" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/la-scala">La Scala</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Exterior_Teatro_Alla_Scala_high_quality_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">John Picken, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Christian communities existed in Mediolanum in Roman times, and the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> with a firm historical presence is Merocles, at the Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> of 313. In the mid-4th century the Arian controversy divided them, and at times there were rival bishops. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambrose">Ambrose</a>, bishop from 374 to 397, quelled the Arians and reshaped the centre, building the basilicas at the city gates: Sant’Ambrogio, San Nazaro in Brolo, San Simpliciano and Sant’Eustorgio. About five million Catholics still use the Ambrosian Rite, which differs slightly from the Roman Rite.</p><p>Water was abundant, so Mediolanum needed no <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aqueduct">aqueducts</a>: springs and nearby rivers and streams supplied it, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> led the Seveso, the Nirone and the Olona towards the moat of the walls. The Navigli, a system of navigable canals, gave the city centre its concentric layout; most are now covered. The street plan still followed the Celtic sanctuary in some places up to the 19th century.</p><p>In the late 18th and 19th centuries Milan was a centre of intellectual and literary life. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cesare-beccaria">Cesare Beccaria</a> wrote <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/on-crimes-and-punishments">Dei delitti e delle pene</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pietro-verri">Pietro Verri</a> published the periodical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/il-caffe">Il Caffè</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alessandro-manzoni">Alessandro Manzoni</a> wrote The Betrothed in the 1820s. Carlo Porta wrote poems in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> language. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-theresa">Maria Theresa</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-scala">Teatro alla Scala</a>, the Brera Academy and the Braidense Library were founded or sponsored. From the 1950s over 400,000 people came from rural southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In 402 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a> besieged Milan, and in 452 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attila">Attila</a> besieged it. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gothic-war-535-554">Gothic War</a> brought the greatest break with the imperial past: in 539 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostrogoths">Ostrogoths</a> conquered and destroyed the city. In 1162 Frederick I Barbarossa brought the destruction of much of Milan. The Milanese had destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lodi-dynasty">Lodi</a>, fought Pavia, Cremona and Como, and captured Empress Beatrice and forced her to ride a donkey backwards through the city. A fire then destroyed the storehouses that held the entire food supply, and within a few days Milan had to surrender.</p><p>Faction followed. In 1252 heretics assassinated the Inquisitor later known as Saint Peter Martyr, and in 1256 the archbishop and leading nobles were expelled. The conflict between the della Torre and the Visconti became a civil war lasting more than a decade. The Great Plague of 1629 to 1631 killed an estimated 60,000 people out of a population of 130,000 and is considered one of the last outbreaks of the plague pandemic that began with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-death">Black Death</a>.</p><p>In May 1898 a riot over the rising cost of living ended in the Bava Beccaris massacre, in which at least 80 demonstrators and two soldiers were killed and 450 wounded, according to government sources. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> Allied bombing caused extensive damage, and German forces occupied the city after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>’s surrender in 1943. On 12 December 1969 a bomb at the National Agrarian Bank in Piazza Fontana killed 17 people and injured 88.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/santa-maria-delle-grazie-milan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/santa-maria-delle-grazie-milan.jpg" alt="Santa Maria delle Grazie, Milan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/santa-maria-delle-grazie-milan">Santa Maria delle Grazie, Milan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Santa_Maria_delle_Grazie_Milan_2013.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Marcin Białek, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>From Mediolanum Constantine issued the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edict-of-milan">Edict of Milan</a> in 313, which granted tolerance to all religions in the Empire. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambrose">Ambrose</a> left the Ambrosian Rite and the churches at the city’s gates. The cathedral, built from 1386 to 1877, is the largest church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and the most important example of Gothic architecture in the country. The Castello Sforzesco grew from a Visconti fortress, and Leonardo painted The Last Supper for the monastery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santa-maria-delle-grazie-milan">Santa Maria delle Grazie</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> crowned himself King of Italy in the cathedral on 26 May 1805.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-scala">Teatro alla Scala</a>, inaugurated in 1778, became a centre of opera with premieres of Bellini, Donizetti, Rossini and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-verdi">Verdi</a>. The Galleria Vittorio Emanuele II was built between 1865 and 1877, and the Arco della Pace was completed in 1838. Corriere della Sera was first published in 1876. After 1945 the city was rebuilt quickly and La Scala reopened in 1946.</p><p>Milan is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a> and the seat of the Metropolitan City of Milan. It has about 1.36 million people in the city proper and is the second-largest city of Italy. Little of the Roman city survives above ground, among it the Colonne di San Lorenzo.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Milan, in the north-western Po Valley, began as the Celtic Medhelanon and became the Roman Mediolanum, an imperial seat under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximian">Maximian</a>. Ruled by the Visconti and Sforza dukes and then by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, it joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy</a> in 1861 and became its industrial and financial centre.</em></p>

